# `BytesSlot`'s hand-written `PartialEq`, answering `true` unconditionally.
#
# The type's whole reason to hand-write `eq` instead of deriving it is to
# compare `read()` — the first `len` bytes — rather than the full backing
# array, so two slots holding the same payload over different tails still
# compare equal. Every test proving that relies only on `assert_eq!`: two
# slots that should be equal, checked to actually be equal. None of them
# would notice a body that answered `true` for every pair, equal or not —
# the same one-polarity shape `readme.md` already names for `is_empty`
# across six other crates, one arity up: a relation asserted on only one of
# its two outcomes is indistinguishable from a constant returning it.
#
# Two slots holding different payloads, checked to compare unequal, is the
# one case that tells them apart.

id: S14
crate: ring_slot
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-09-05
round-failures: 1
detector: slots_with_different_payloads_are_not_equal

--- from
    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
        self.read() == other.read()
--- to
    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
        true // PROBE ONLY — G12, was `self.read() == other.read()`
--- end
